      "content": "You have correctly identified the pattern. By mapping each $x$-coordinate to the mark and each $y$-coordinate to the frequency, we get the following table:\n\n| Mark | Frequency |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| 0 | 0 |\n| 1 | 3 |\n| 2 | 4 |\n| 3 | 5 |\n| 4 | 3 |\n| 5 | 6 |\n| 6 | 0 |\n| 7 | 7 |\n| 8 | 9 |\n| 9 | 5 |\n| 10 | 0 |\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Concept:** $P(x; y) \\rightarrow (\\text{Mark}; \\text{Frequency})$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $x$: Represents the specific category or value (the mark).\n  * $y$: Represents the count or occurrence of that value (the frequency).\n* **Why we use it:** Graphs are visual summaries. Converting them back to a table allows us to perform further calculations like the mean, median, or mode more easily.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always check if the frequency is 0 for certain marks. Students often accidentally skip marks where the frequency is 0, but it is important to include them in your table to show you have read the graph accurately."
    }
